You're right that most things convert to an ugly number of pounds /
ounces. I'm not really sure how best to deal with that. I actually
choose the kg conversion factor to be 50g per unit so that there would
be lots of nice round numbers... when in actual fact, 40g per unit
would probably be slightly more realistic. But for pounds to be
consistent with that, the conversion is 1 unit =  1.76 oz... which
obviously will not produce nice round numbers - so it makes me doubt
the validity of the original conversion factor. So... I'm thinking
that I should either forget about round numbers, and change the kg
conversion to 40g per unit (and update the pounds conversion
accordingly); or I should forget about making the units consistent,
and just use 1.6 oz per unit or something like that... which would
produce more round numbers. (For example, potions would weigh exactly
2lb.)


In other news. I've got a tip for playing this mod. The tip is that
you're going to want to carry a ring of regen if you can find one. In
the later parts of the game, I've found that natural regen isn't quite
enough to keep you at full health. So you can get gradually worn down
by all the little battles you have to fight and eventually find
yourself in a bit of trouble. (which is exactly the kind of thing I
had in mind when I changed the regen formula). In the game I'm playing
now I've got myself into the uncomfortable situation where I have no
ring of regen, and I've run out of healing potions, and I'm at 20hp...
and I've been diving deeper into gehennom with a wand of digging to
escape from some fights. It's a pretty dire situation. In vanilla, I'd
be full health again in just a handful of turns thanks to natural
regen being huge; but in this, it is not huge. For those interested in
the numbers, I calculate that the old regen for this particular
character would have been around 3 hp/turn; and the new regen is
around 0.5 hp per turn.


